## Data Source

```C
#include &lt;SilikoCore/DataSource.h&gt;
```

### `SilikoDataSource` — Opaque Type

The `SilikoDataSource` type represents a user-provided source of input
characters.

```C
struct SilikoDataSource;
typedef struct SilikoDataSource SilikoDataSource;
```

### `SilikoDataSourceNew` — Function

The `SilikoDataSourceNew` function creates a new `SilikoDataSource`
object.

```C
SilikoDataSource *SilikoDataSourceNew(
	void *NewState,
	int (*NewAdvanceFunction)(void *),
	char (*NewGetFunction)(void *),
	void (*NewDeleteFunction)(void *)
);
```

* `NewState` is a pointer to an object representing the state of the
  data source (the *state object*). `NewState` should be fully
  initialized and ready to use before being passed to
  `SilikoDataSourceNew`.
* `NewAdvanceFunction` is a pointer to a function (the *advance
  function*) that accepts the state object (as a `void` pointer) and
  advance the state object to the next character if there are further
  characters to read from the input. The function must return true if
  there are further characters to read, and must return false if the
  input source is exhausted.
* `NewGetFunction` is a pointer to a function (the *get function*) that
  takes the state object (as a `void` pointer) and returns the current
  character of the source if the source is not exhausted, and returns
  `\0` if the source is exhausted. The function must be idempotent until
  the advance function is called on the state object.
* `NewDeleteFunction` is a pointer to a function (the *delete function*)
  that takes the state object (as a `void` pointer), and frees up any
  resources used by the state object. The function must also take care
  of dealocating the state object itself if applicable.

**Return Value:** If successful, `SilikoDataSourceNew` returns a newly
allocated `SilikoDataSource` object using the provided state object and
function pointers. If there was an error, `SilikoDataSourceNew` returns
`NULL`. If `SilikoDataSourceNew` returns `NULL`, The state object is
*not* deleted.

### `SilikoDataSourceAdvance` — Function

The `SilikoDataSourceAdvance` function advances a `SilikoDataSource`
object to the Next character of input by calling the advance function
of the object.

```C
int SilikoDataSourceAdvance(SilikoDataSource *Source);
```

* `Source` is the input source to advance. `Source` must be a value
  returned by `SiolikoDataSourceNew`.

**Return value:** `SiikoDataSourceAdvance` returns the return value of
the call to the advance function of the passed `SilikoDataSource` object
(true if the object has more characters to read, or false if the object
is exhausted).

### `SilikoDataSourceGet` — Function

The `SilikoDataSourceGet` function gets the current character from a
`SilikoDataSource` object by calling the get function of the object.

```C
char SilikoDataSourceGet(SilikoDataSource *Source);
```

* `Source` is the input source to advance. `Source` must be a value
  returned by `SiolikoDataSourceNew`.

**Return value:** `SilikoDataSourceGet` returns return value of the
call to the get function of the `SilikoDataSource` object (the current
character if the object has characters to read, or `\0` if the source is
exhausted).

### `SilikoDataSourceDelete` — Function

The `SilikoDataSourceDelete` function calls the delete function of a
`SiikoDataSource` object and then deletes the object.

```C
void SilikoDataSourceDelete(SilikoDataSource *Source);
```

* `Source` is the input source to advance. `Source` must be a value
  returned by `SiolikoDataSourceNew`.


## Function Caller

```C
#include &lt;SilikoCore/FunctionCaller.h&gt;
```

### `SilikoFunctionCaller` — Opaque Type

A `SilikoFunctionCaller` is a lookup table for functions.

```C
struct SilikoFunctionCaller;
typedef struct SilikoFunctionCaller SilikoFunctionCaller;
```

### SilikoFunctionPointer — Alias for a Function Pointer

A `SilikoFunctionPointer` points to a function used by Siliko Core to
implement functions and operators.

```C
typedef SilikoValue (*SilikoFunctionPointer)(
    int /* ArgC */,
    SilikoValue * /* ArgV */);
```
* `ArgC` is the number of arguments being passed to the implementation.
* `ArgV` is a pointer to the first element in an array of `SilikoValue`
  objects being passed to the function as arguments.

**Return Value:** The function pointed to returns the result of the
function or operator it implements, or an error if it encounters one.

### `SilikoFunctionCallerNew` — Function

The `SilikoFunctionCallerNew` function creates a new
`SilikoFunctionCaller` object.

```C
SilikoFunctionCaller *SilikoFunctionCallerNew();
```

**ReturnValue:** Unless there is a memory error,
`SilikoFunctionCallerNew` returns a pointer to the new object. If there
is a memory error, `SilikoFunctionCallerNew` returns `NULL`. If
`SilikoFunctionCallerNew` returns a pointer to an object, it must be
deleted with `SilikoFunctionCallerDelete`.

### `SilikoFunctionCallerDelete` — Function

The `SilikoFunctionCallerDelete` function deletes a
`SilikoFunctionCaller` object.

```C
void SilikoFunctionCallerDelete(SilikoFunctionCaller *Caller);
```

* `Caller` is the `SilikoFunctionCaller` object to destroy.

### `SilikoFunctionCallerInstallOpertors` — Function

The `SilikoFunctionCallerInstallOperators` function installs the
operators used by the infix parser to handle operators.

```C
int SilikoFunctionCallerInstallOperators(SilikoFunctionCaller *Caller);
```

* `Caller` is the `SilikoFunctionCaller` object to install the functions
  into.

**Return Value:** `SilikoFunctionCallerInstallOperators` returns true
if all functions were installed successfully, `NULL` otherwise. If
`NULL` was returned, then the selection of functions installed in the
`SilikoFunctionCaller` object is unspecified.

### `SilikoFunctionCallerInstallFunctions` — Function

The `SilikoFunctionCallerInstallFunctions` function Installs the
functions supporting the C `math.h` functions.

```C
int SilikoFunctionCallerInstallFunctions(SilikoFunctionCaller *Caller);
```

* `Caller` is the `SilikoFunctionCaller` object to install the functions
  into.

**Return Value:** `SilikoFunctionCallerInstallFunctionss` returns true
if all functions were installed successfully, `NULL` otherwise. If
`NULL` was returned, then the selection of functions installed in the
`SilikoFunctionCaller` object is unspecified.

### `SilikoFunctionCallerInstall` — Function

The `SilikoFunctionCallerInstall` installs a single function into a
`SilikoFunctionCaller` object.

```C
int SilikoFunctionCallerInstall(
    SilikoFunctionCaller *Caller,
    const char *FunctionName,
    SilikoFunctionPointer Function
);
```

* `Caller` is the `SilikoFunctionCaller` object to install the function
  into.
* `FunctionName` is a string that will be used as the identifier for
  the installed function.
* `Function` is a pointer to a C function implementing the Siliko
  function being installed.

**ReturnValue:** Returns true if the installation succeeded, false
otherwise.

### `SilikoFunctionCallerCall` — Function

Call a function installed in the function caller.

```C
SilikoValue SilikoFunctionCallerCall(
    SilikoFunctionCaller *Caller,
    const char *FunctionName,
    int ArgumentCount,
    SilikoValue *Arguments
);
```

* `Caller` is the function caller in which to look up the function.
* `FunctionName` is the identifier of the function to call.
*` ArgumentCount` is the number of arguments being passed to the
  function.
* `Arguments` is a pointer to the first element in an array of
  `SilikoValue` objects being passed to the function. It must contain
  at least as many elements as are specified by `ArgumentCount`.

## Infix Parser

```C
#include &lt;SilikoCore/InfixParser.h&gt;
```

### `SilikoParseInfix` — Function

The `SilikoParseInfix` function parses the input from a
`SilikoDataSourcce` and generates a syntax tree representing the parsed
input.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoParseInfix(SilikoDataSource *input);
```

* `input` is the data source from which the parser will get its input.
  The parser will take ownership of the data source, and call
  `SilikoDataSourceDelete` on it when the parser is finished with its
  input.

**Return Value:** `SilikoParseInfix` returns the root node of the
syntax tree generated from parsing the input from the input source.

## Lexer

```C
#include &lt;SilikoCore/Lexer.h&gt;
```

### `SilikoLexer` — Opaque Type

A `SilikoLexer` is a tool used to lexically analize input from a
`SilikoInputSource` and convert the input to a sequence of meaningful
tokens.

```C
struct SilikoLexer;
typedef struct SilikoLexer SilikoLexer;
```

### `SilikoLexerNew` — Function

The `SilikoLexerNew` function creates a new `SilikoLexer` object.

```C
SilikoLexer *SilikoLexerNew(SilikoDataSource *InputSource);
```

* `InputSource` is the data source that will be used for input. The
  lexer will take ownership of the `SilikoDataSource` and call
  `SilikoDataSourceDeleve` on the object when the lexer is finished
  with the data source.

**Return Value:** `SilikoLexerNew` returns a pointer to a new
`SiikoLexer` object if there was no memory error. `SilikoLexerNew`
returns `NULL` if there was a memory error.

### `SilikoLexerDelete` — Function

The `SilikoLexerDelete` function deletes a `SilikoLexer` object.

```C
void SilikoLexerDelete(SilikoLexer *Lexer);
```

* `Lexer` is the lexer to delete.

### `SilikoLexerNext` — Function

The `SilikoLexerNext` function advances the `SilikoLexer` object to its
next token.

```C
void SilikoLexerNext(SilikoLexer *Lexer);
```

* `Lexer` is the lexer to advance. If `Lexer` has reached the end of its
  input, further calls to `SilikoLexerNext` on `Lexer` will have no
  effect, and `SilikoLexerGetToken` will return a token indicating the
  end of input has been reached.

### `SilikoLexerGetToken` — Function

The `SilikoLexerGetToken` function gets the current token from the
lexer.

```C
SilikoToken SilikoLexerGetToken(SilikoLexer *Lexer);
```

* `Lexer` is the `SilikoLexer` object from which to retrieve the token.

**Return Value:** A SilikoToken object representing the curent token.
If the `Type` property of the return value is `SILIKO_TOK_ID`, the
`Id` will be a pointer to a string that is owned by the `Lexer`, and
will only be available until the next time `SilikoTokenNext` is called 
on `Lexer`. If the `Type` property of the return value is
`SILIKO_TOK_EOL`, then further calls to `SilikoLexerNext` will have no
effect on `Lexer`.

## String Source

```C
#include &lt;SilikoCore/StringSource.h&gt;
```

### `SiikoStringSourceNew` — Function

The `SiikoStringSourceNew` calls `SiikoInputSourceNew` to create a
`SiikoInputSource` that reads from a string.

```C
SilikoDataSource *SilikoStringSourceNew(const char *InputString);
```

* `InputString` is a string to read input characters from.
  `SiikoStringSourceNew` copies the string, so the InputString passed
  is still managed by the caller.

**Return Value:** If successful, `SiikoStringSourceNew` returns pointer
to a new `SilikoInputSource` object returned by `SilikoInputSourceNew`.
On failure, `SiikoStringSourceNew` retruns `NULL`. 

## Syntax Tree

```C
#include &lt;SilikoCore/SyntaxTree.h&gt;
```

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNodeType` — Enumeration

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeNodeType` represents whether a syntax tree node
is a branch, leaf, or a simple placeholder.

```C
enum SilikoSyntaxTreeNodeType
{
	SILIKO_AST_NOTHING = 0,
	SILIKO_AST_BRANCH,
	SILIKO_AST_LEAF
};
```

* `SILIKO_AST_NOTHING` represents a syntax tree node that was generated
  by a parser to indicate that a rule was not able to process further
  input, but is not in an error condition. This is used, for example,
  to indicate that the end of a list has been reached by a function that
  does not process the end of list indicator or the next operator being
  processed.
* `SILIKO_AST_BRANCH` represents a syntax tree that is a branch. A
  branch node will have an operator name, and an array of child
  `SyntaxTreeNode` objects.
* `SILIKO_AST_LEAF` represents a syntax tree that is a leaf. The node
  has no child nodes, and is either a value or an error.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` — Opaque Type

A `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object is a node in a syntax tree generated
by a parser.

```C
struct SilikoSyntaxTreeNode;
typedef struct SilikoSyntaxTreeNode SilikoSyntaxTreeNode;
```

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewLeaf` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewLeaf` function generates a new leaf node from
a `SilikoValue` object.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoSyntaxTreeNewLeaf(SilikoValue Value);
```

* `Value` is the value to be stored in the leaf node.

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewLeaf` returns a pointer to a new
leaf `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object with the value passed on success,
or `NULL` if there was a memory error.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewFromInteger` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewFromInteger` function generates a new leaf node
from an integer value.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoSyntaxTreeNewFromInteger(long long int IntegerValue);
```

* `IntegerValue` is the value to be stored in the leaf node.

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewFromInteger` returns a pointer to
a new leaf `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object with the value passed on success, or
`NULL` if there was a memory error.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewFromFloat` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewFromFloat` function generates a new leaf node
from a floating-point value.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoSyntaxTreeNewFromFloat(double FloatValue);
```

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewFromFloat` returns a pointer to a
new leaf `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object with the value passed on
success, or `NULL` if there was a memory error.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewBranch` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewBranch` function creates a new branch
`SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoSyntaxTreeNewBranch(char *Id);
```

* `Id` is the function identifier used by this branch node.

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewBranch` returns a pointer to a
new branch `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object with the function identifier
passed on success, or `NULL` if there was a memory error.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewError` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewError` function creates new
`SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object representing a syntax error.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoSyntaxTreeNewError(void);
```

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewError` returns a pointer to a
new error `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object on success, or `NULL` if there
was a memory error.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewNothing` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewNothing` function creates a new
`SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object representing that the end of input for
a particular rule has been reached, and that further parsing should be
done by another rule, such as a parent rule.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoSyntaxTreeNewNothing(void);
```

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeNewError` returns a pointer to a
new empty `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object on success, or `NULL` if there
was a memory error.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeDelete` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeDelete` function deletes a syntax tree node,
and if the `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object is a branch node, deletes its
child nodes and any other descendant nodes.

```C
void SilikoSyntaxTreeDelete(SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SyntaxTree);
```

* `SyntaxTree` is the `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` object to delete. 

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeGetType` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeGetType` function gets the type of the
`SyntaxTreeNodeObject`.

```C
enum SilikoSyntaxTreeNodeType SilikoSyntaxTreeGetType(SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SyntaxTree);
```

* `SyntaxTree` is the syntax tree node to prove for its type.

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeGetType` returns a value indicating
whether the syntax tree node is a leaf, branch, error, or nothing.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeIsError` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeIsError` probes a syntax tree node for whether it
is an error node or not.

```C
int SilikoSyntaxTreeIsError(SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SyntaxTree);
```

* `SyntaxTree` is the syntax tree node to probe.

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeIsError` returns true if the node
represents a syntax error.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeNegate` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreeNegate` function negates the value of a syntax
tree node.

```C
int SilikoSyntaxTreeNegate(SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SyntaxTree);
```

* `SyntaxTree` is the syntax tree to negate.

**ReturnValue:** `SilikoSyntaxTreeNegate` returns true if the node was
a valid branch or leaf node that could be negated, false otherwise.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreePushLeft` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreePushLeft` function adds a child to a branch
node, to the left of any existing child nodes.

```C
int SilikoSyntaxTreePushLeft(
    SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *BaseTree,
    SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *NewBranch
);
```

* `BaseTree` is the tree to which the new node is added.
* `NewBranch` is the new child node to add.

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreePushLeft` returns true if the
operation was successful, or false if the insertion failed.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreePushRight` — Function

The `SilikoSyntaxTreePushRight` function adds a child to a branch
node, to the right of any existing child nodes.

```C
int SilikoSyntaxTreePushRight(
    SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *BaseTree,
    SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *NewBranch
);
```

* `BaseTree` is the tree to which the new node is added.
* `NewBranch` is the new child node to add.

**Return Value:** `SilikoSyntaxTreePushRight` returns true if the
operation was successful, or false if the insertion failed.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeGetCount` — Function

Gets the number of children branch node has.

```C
int SilikoSyntaxTreeGetCount(
    SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *Parent,
);
```

* `Parent` is the node from which to retrieve the number of children.

**Return value:** If `Parent` is a branch node, the return value is the
number of child nodes the `Parent` has. Otherwise, the return value is
-1 to indicate that `Parent` is not a branch.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreeGetChild` — Function

Gets the child of the specified `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` at the specified
position, leaving the child in its place.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoSyntaxTreeGetChild(
    SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *Parent,
    int Position
);
```

* `Parent` is a node to get a child node from.
* `Position` is the position of the requested child. If the value is
  non-negative, the `Position` is interpreted as an index counting from
  the left. If the value is negative, `Position` is interpreted as a
  n index counting from the right, with `-1` as the left element, and
  so on. If the value passed would count past the end of the list of
  child nodes, the value is considered out of range, otherwise the value
  is considered in range.

**Return value:** If `parent` is a branch node, and `position` is in
range, this function returns a pointer to the child. Otherwise, this
function returns `NULL`. This pointer is still managed by the parent
and should not be passed to `SilikoSyntaxTreeDelete` or added as a child
to another branch node.

### `SilikoSyntaxTreePruneChild` — Function

Gets the child of the specified `SilikoSyntaxTreeNode` at the specified
position, removing the child from the parent.

```C
SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *SilikoSyntaxTreePruneChild(
    SilikoSyntaxTreeNode *Parent,
    int Position
);
```

* `Parent` is a node to get a child node from.
* `Position` is the position of the requested child. If the value is
  non-negative, the `Position` is interpreted as an index counting from
  the left. If the value is negative, `Position` is interpreted as a
  n index counting from the right, with `-1` as the left element, and
  so on. If the value passed would count past the end of the list of
  child nodes, the value is considered out of range, otherwise the value
  is considered in range.

**Return value:** If `parent` is a branch node, and `position` is in
range, this function returns a pointer to the child. Otherwise, this
function returns `NULL`. This pointer is no longer managed by the parent
and must be passed to `SilikoSyntaxTreeDelete` or added as a child
to another branch node to avoid a memory leak.
